les are saturated (capacities are"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"finite). A self-loop carries its capacity when its cost is negative and nothing otherwise."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Network simplex with block-search pivots and strongly feasible trees, LEMON’s layout."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"supply"},{"type":"text","text":" is called once per vertex in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices"},{"type":"text","text":" order, then, for each edge in position"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"order, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"capacity"},{"type":"text","text":" and then "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"cost"},{"type":"text","text":", before any work. Which optimum is returned when several"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"exist is unspecified, but the same input always gives the same result."}],"type":"topic","title":"minimumCostFlow(supply:capacity:cost:)"},"doc://Flows/documentation/Flows/GraphProtocols/DirectedGraph/vertexConnectivity(from:to:)":{"title":"vertexConnectivity(from:to:)","identifier":"doc:\/\/Flows\/documentation\/Flows\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedGraph\/vertexConnectivity(from:to:)","kind":"symbol","type":"topic","role":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The most internally vertex-disjoint paths from "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"source"},{"type":"text","text":" to "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"target"},{"type":"text","text":"; an edge"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"source → target counts as one path, whatever its multiplicity (NetworkX)."}],"url":"\/documentation\/flows\/graphprotocols\/directedgraph\/vertexconnectivity(from:to:)","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"func"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"vertexConnectivity"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"kind":"externalParam","text":"from"},{"kind":"text","text":": "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Self"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}